{
  "summary": "The terminal_stage proposal has three underexplored weaknesses. First, it only handles truncation (stopping early) but not the equally common need to skip early phases — a board that wants to skip ideation and start directly at planning cannot express this with terminal_stage alone, requiring a separate skip_phases or start_stage mechanism that fragments the configuration model. Second, the proposal assumes the pipeline entry point always begins at plan-inbox (line 1485), but _run_pipeline_impl's phase checks are positional, not declarative — if an item is manually placed into an intermediate stage on a board whose terminal_stage is before that stage, the pipeline will silently run past the terminal point because the check only fires after stage completion, not before stage entry. Third, routing to final-human-approval on terminal_stage hit creates a UX confusion: the human reviewer in final-human-approval currently sees items that have been through implementation and testing, with code changes to review. A plan-only item arriving in final-human-approval has fundamentally different review semantics (reviewing a plan, not code), but the WebUI and TUI present final-human-approval identically regardless of what work was actually done. Without per-item context about which terminal_stage was hit, the reviewer has no way to know what they're approving. The proposal needs either a richer final-human-approval view that adapts based on completed phases, or a distinct approval phase for early-terminated items."
}
